    {
      "step_id": "tile_sql_case_expressions.s02",
      "type": "explainer",
      "concept_ids": [
        "sql.case_expressions"
      ],
      "eyebrow": "ELSE and the simple form",
      "title": "What happens when no WHEN matches — and the simple CASE form",
      "lede": "If no condition is true, CASE returns the ELSE value. Omit ELSE and it returns NULL. There is also a shorter form for equality checks.",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "prose",
          "text": "If no WHEN condition is true, CASE returns the ELSE result if given, or NULL if ELSE is omitted. A row with a NULL delivery_time_minutes also falls through to ELSE, since no WHEN condition is true for a null. Omitting ELSE can silently produce NULLs.",
          "role": "mechanism",
          "lineage": {
            "concept_ids": [
              "sql.case_expressions"
            ],
            "fact_ids": [
              "sql.case_expressions#f2"
            ],
            "teaching_block_ids": [
              "sql.case_expressions#tb3"
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "prose",
          "text": "CASE also has a simpler form for equality comparisons: `CASE expression WHEN value THEN result … END` compares one expression against each listed value directly. `CASE city WHEN 'Lagos' THEN 'south' WHEN 'Kano' THEN 'north' ELSE 'other' END` compares city to each city value instead of writing a full condition each time. Use this form when all branches test the same expression for equality.",
          "role": "syntax",
          "lineage": {
            "concept_ids": [
              "sql.case_expressions"
            ],
            "fact_ids": [
              "sql.case_expressions#f3"
            ],
            "teaching_block_ids": [
              "sql.case_expressions#tb3"
            ]
          }
        }
      ],
      "reward": {
        "gems": 5
      },
      "lineage": {
        "concept_ids": [
          "sql.case_expressions"
        ],
        "fact_ids": [
          "sql.case_expressions#f2",
          "sql.case_expressions#f3"
        ],
        "teaching_block_ids": [
          "sql.case_expressions#tb3"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "step_id": "tile_sql_case_expressions.s03",
      "type": "check",
      "concept_ids": [
        "sql.case_expressions"
      ],
      "eyebrow": "Quick check",
      "title": "Writing CASE with ordered WHEN branches",
      "lede": "Confirm you understand that WHEN conditions are tested in order and the first match wins.",
      "reward": {
        "gems": 5
      },
      "lineage": {
        "concept_ids": [
          "sql.case_expressions"
        ],
        "fact_ids": [
          "sql.case_expressions#f1"
        ],
        "teaching_block_ids": [],
        "anchors": [
          "sql.case_expressions#assessment:write_case_with_ordered_when_branches"
        ]
      },
      "interaction": {
        "type": "choice",
        "interaction_id": "ix.tile_sql_case_expressions.s03",
        "difficulty": "intro",
        "grading_mode": "client",
        "standalone": false,
        "measures": [
          {
            "concept_id": "sql.case_expressions",
            "capability": "write_case_with_ordered_when_branches",
            "fact_ids": [
              "f1"
            ]
          }
        ],
        "anchors": [
          "sql.case_expressions#assessment:write_case_with_ordered_when_branches"
        ],
        "public": {
          "prompt": "A CASE expression has three WHEN branches:\n```sql\nCASE\n  WHEN amount >= 5000 THEN 'high'\n  WHEN amount >= 3000 THEN 'medium'\n  WHEN amount >= 1000 THEN 'low'\nEND\n```\nAn order has `amount = 6000`. What label does this expression return?",
          "options": [
            {
              "option_id": "opt_a",
              "text": "'high', because the first WHEN condition that is true is returned."
            },
            {
              "option_id": "opt_b",
              "text": "'medium', because 6000 also satisfies `amount >= 3000`."
            },
            {
              "option_id": "opt_c",
              "text": "'low', because 6000 also satisfies `amount >= 1000`."
            },
            {
              "option_id": "opt_d",
              "text": "An error, because 6000 matches more than one WHEN condition."
            }
          ],
          "selection": "single",
          "shuffle": true
        },
        "grading": {
          "correct_option_ids": [
            "opt_a"
          ],
          "option_feedback": {
            "opt_a": {
              "message": "Correct. CASE evaluates WHEN conditions in order and returns the result of the first one that is true. `amount >= 5000` is true for 6000, so 'high' is returned immediately."
            },
            "opt_b": {
              "message": "Incorrect. CASE stops at the first true condition. `amount >= 5000` is true for 6000, so 'high' is returned before the second WHEN is even checked."
            },
            "opt_c": {
              "message": "Incorrect. CASE stops at the first true condition. The third WHEN is never reached because the first WHEN already matched."
            },
            "opt_d": {
              "message": "Incorrect. CASE does not error when multiple conditions could match. It simply returns the result of the first true WHEN and ignores the rest."
            }
          }
        },
        "feedback": {
          "correct": {
            "message": "CASE evaluates its WHEN branches in order and returns the result of the first true condition. For amount = 6000, the first branch matches and 'high' is returned."
          },
          "incorrect": {
            "message": "CASE evaluates WHEN conditions from top to bottom and stops at the first one that is true. Even if later conditions would also be true, they are never evaluated."
          },
          "hints": [
            "Think of CASE like a series of if/else-if checks: the first match wins.",
            "Trace through the conditions in order: is `amount >= 5000` true for 6000?"
          ],
          "remediation_step_id": "tile_sql_case_expressions.r01"
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "step_id": "tile_sql_case_expressions.s04",
      "type": "check",
      "concept_ids": [
        "sql.case_expressions"
