      "eyebrow": "Let's revisit",
      "title": "WHEN conditions are tested in order — first match wins",
      "lede": "The order of WHEN branches matters: SQL stops at the first true condition.",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "prose",
          "text": "A CASE expression evaluates its WHEN conditions from top to bottom and returns the result of the first one that is true. Once a branch matches, the remaining branches are not tested. In `CASE WHEN delivery_time_minutes < 20 THEN 'fast' WHEN delivery_time_minutes < 40 THEN 'normal' ELSE 'slow' END`, an order with delivery_time_minutes = 15 matches the first branch and is labelled 'fast' — the second branch is never evaluated for that row.",
          "role": "mechanism",
